#!/usr/bin/env python3
import json
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path('content/articles/en')

ADDITIONS = {
16: """<h2>Protein per calorie: why nuts are not a lean protein source</h2><p>The ranking looks impressive by weight, but nuts are energy-dense. One ounce of almonds provides roughly <strong>6 g of protein and about 160–165 calories</strong>; an ounce of pistachios provides about <strong>6 g and roughly 160 calories</strong>. For comparison, 100 g of cooked chicken breast is in a similar calorie range—around 165 calories—but provides about <strong>31 g of protein</strong>. That does not make almonds or pistachios inferior foods; it shows that their nutritional role is different.</p><p>Nuts combine protein with unsaturated fat, fiber and micronutrients. They are excellent for adding a few grams of protein to breakfast, snacks or salads, but they are much less efficient if the only goal is maximizing protein for a fixed calorie budget.</p><h2>Which nut makes the most sense in a normal serving?</h2><p>The top of the table is close. A one-ounce serving of almonds gives about <strong>6 g of protein</strong>, pistachios about <strong>5.5–6 g</strong> and cashews about <strong>5 g</strong>. Choosing almonds over pistachios for a difference of a few tenths of a gram is unlikely to matter in practice.</p><p>Other nutrients can be a better tie-breaker. Walnuts provide ALA omega-3; almonds are notable for vitamin E; pistachios contribute potassium and vitamin B6; cashews provide copper, magnesium and zinc. A varied mix often makes more nutritional sense than repeatedly buying whichever nut sits first in a protein ranking.</p><h2>What about nut butters?</h2><p>Grinding a nut into butter does not remove its protein, but serving size changes how easy it is to eat a large amount. Two tablespoons of almond butter typically provide around <strong>6–7 g of protein</strong> and roughly <strong>190–200 calories</strong>, depending on the product. Added sugar, oils and salt can also change the label.</p><p>If protein is the reason for buying a nut butter, compare products per two-tablespoon serving rather than assuming all jars are equivalent. A short ingredient list also makes it easier to understand what you are actually comparing.</p><h2>Brazil nuts are a special case</h2><p>Brazil nuts are not especially high in protein compared with almonds or pistachios, but they are extremely rich in selenium. That means eating large quantities simply to chase more protein is a poor trade-off. Their value comes from a distinctive micronutrient profile, not from winning the protein table.</p><h2>Bottom line</h2><p>Among common tree nuts, almonds and pistachios are the strongest protein options at roughly <strong>20–21 g per 100 g</strong>, or about <strong>6 g per ounce</strong>. Cashews follow at around 18 g per 100 g. The practical difference between the leaders is small, so portion size, calories and the rest of the nutrient profile matter more than the exact podium.</p>""",
17: """<h2>What one ounce actually gives you</h2><p>An ounce is a more useful comparison than 100 g for most meals. Hulled hemp provides about <strong>9 g of protein per ounce</strong>, pumpkin seeds roughly <strong>8–9 g</strong>, sunflower seeds about <strong>6 g</strong>, flax and sesame around <strong>5 g</strong>, and chia roughly <strong>4–5 g</strong>. That is enough to change the protein content of a bowl of oatmeal or yogurt, but it is still smaller than a full serving of tofu, beans, fish or poultry.</p><h2>Protein per calorie changes the picture</h2><p>Seeds commonly provide around <strong>140–170 calories per ounce</strong> because they contain substantial oil. Hemp and pumpkin seeds therefore deliver a useful amount of protein, but they are not equivalent to a very lean protein source. The same calories from cooked chicken breast, egg whites or a lean white fish can provide considerably more protein.</p><p>This is not a criticism of seeds. Their fat includes unsaturated fatty acids, and they also provide fiber and minerals. It simply explains why a tablespoon of seeds works best as an addition to a meal rather than as the entire protein base.</p><h2>Hemp versus pumpkin seeds</h2><p>These two are close enough that the choice does not need to be made on protein alone. Hemp is around <strong>31–32 g of protein per 100 g</strong>, while pumpkin seeds are around <strong>30 g</strong>. In a one-ounce serving, the difference is roughly a gram or less.</p><p>Hemp contributes polyunsaturated fats, while pumpkin seeds stand out for magnesium, zinc and iron. Rotating them gives more variety than treating one as nutritionally superior.</p><h2>Chia and flax: lower protein, different strengths</h2><p>Chia provides roughly <strong>16–17 g of protein per 100 g</strong>, well below hemp and pumpkin, but its fiber content is exceptionally high. Flax is around <strong>18 g</strong> and is notable for ALA omega-3. These differences show why a protein ranking should not be used as a general nutrition score.</p><p>Ground flax is usually easier to incorporate into food and makes the nutrients inside the seed more accessible. Chia absorbs water and forms a gel, which changes texture and can make a small serving feel substantial.</p><h2>Roasted, salted and flavored seeds</h2><p>Roasting can shift the per-100-g numbers slightly by changing moisture, but it does not remove the protein. Added salt can raise sodium dramatically, while sugar, chocolate coatings or added oils can change calories and the overall nutrient profile much more than roasting itself.</p><h2>Bottom line</h2><p>If protein is the only metric, hulled hemp and pumpkin seeds are the clear leaders among common seeds at around <strong>30–32 g per 100 g</strong>. In realistic servings they contribute about <strong>8–9 g per ounce</strong>. Sunflower, flax, sesame and chia add smaller amounts while bringing different combinations of fiber, minerals and fats.</p>""",
22: """<h2>A simple decision guide</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Potato condition</th><th>Practical choice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Firm, no green areas, one or two tiny sprouts</td><td>Remove sprouts, eyes and surrounding peel generously; use promptly</td></tr><tr><td>Many long sprouts</td><td>Discard</td></tr><tr><td>Green skin or green flesh</td><td>Discard</td></tr><tr><td>Very soft, shriveled or rotten</td><td>Discard</td></tr><tr><td>Noticeably bitter or causes oral burning</td><td>Do not continue eating; discard</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The table is intentionally conservative. A home cook cannot measure glycoalkaloid concentration by looking at a potato, so appearance, texture and storage history are used as practical signals.</p><h2>Sprouts are more than a quality issue</h2><p>As a potato breaks dormancy, it uses stored carbohydrate to grow shoots. The sprouts themselves and the tissue around the eyes can contain much higher concentrations of glycoalkaloids than the inner flesh. That is why simply snapping off the visible tip is not enough; the eye and surrounding peel should also be removed when the rest of the potato is still firm and non-green.</p><p>A heavily sprouted potato is a different situation. Once there are many long shoots, substantial shriveling or green tissue, there is little reason to try to rescue it. The food-quality loss and uncertainty about glycoalkaloids outweigh the value of saving one potato.</p><h2>How much can peeling and cooking reduce glycoalkaloids?</h2><p>EFSA reports wide ranges rather than one fixed number. <strong>Peeling can reduce glycoalkaloids by about 25–75%</strong>, boiling by roughly <strong>5–65%</strong>, and frying in oil by around <strong>20–90%</strong>. Those ranges are broad because the starting concentration, potato variety and processing conditions differ.</p><p>This is why cooking should not be treated as a rescue guarantee. A 25% reduction is very different from a 75% reduction, and neither tells you the starting concentration in a particular heavily sprouted potato.</p><h2>What does the 1 mg/kg adverse-effect level mean?</h2><p>EFSA identified <strong>1 mg of glycoalkaloids per kilogram of body weight per day</strong> as the lowest observed adverse effect level in its assessment. For a 70 kg adult, that corresponds mathematically to 70 mg. It is not a household “safe allowance,” because you do not know how many milligrams are present in a suspicious potato and sensitivity varies.</p><p>The number is useful for understanding why children can be more vulnerable: the same amount of glycoalkaloids represents a larger dose per kilogram of body weight in a smaller person.</p><h2>How to slow sprouting at home</h2><p>Keep potatoes in a dark, dry and ventilated place and avoid prolonged light exposure. Buy quantities you can realistically use before they become soft and heavily sprouted. Check the bag periodically and remove rotten potatoes so they do not accelerate deterioration of the rest.</p><h2>Bottom line</h2><p>A tiny sprout on an otherwise firm, non-green potato is not the same as a potato covered in long shoots. The first can often be used after generous trimming; the second should be discarded. Greening, severe shriveling, rot and bitterness all push the decision toward throwing the potato away rather than relying on cooking to fix it.</p>""",
23: """<h2>A quick decision guide</h2><table><thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>What it means in practice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Normal skin color, firm potato</td><td>Use normally if otherwise sound</td></tr><tr><td>Clearly green skin or flesh</td><td>Discard</td></tr><tr><td>Green plus many sprouts or shriveling</td><td>Discard</td></tr><tr><td>Bitter taste or oral burning</td><td>Stop eating and discard</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The green pigment is chlorophyll, not solanine. The reason green potatoes are treated cautiously is that light exposure can stimulate chlorophyll and glycoalkaloid formation at the same time.</p><h2>Why green color cannot tell you the exact toxin level</h2><p>Two potatoes with the same shade of green do not necessarily contain the same amount of glycoalkaloids. Variety, storage conditions, damage and the depth of the green tissue all matter. Conversely, a potato does not need to look intensely green to contain some glycoalkaloids.</p><p>That uncertainty is exactly why visible greening is used as a practical warning rather than as a home measurement. EFSA consumer guidance recommends not eating green potatoes instead of asking people to estimate a concentration from color.</p><h2>How much do peeling and cooking reduce glycoalkaloids?</h2><p>Processing can reduce them, but the range is wide. EFSA reports reductions of approximately <strong>25–75% from peeling</strong>, <strong>5–65% from boiling</strong> and <strong>20–90% from frying in oil</strong>. A wide range means there is no reliable household calculation such as “boiling removes half, so this green potato is safe.”</p><p>For a normal, non-green potato, those processing effects are part of ordinary cooking. For a clearly green potato, they are not a good reason to override the simpler advice to discard it.</p><h2>What does the 1 mg/kg figure mean?</h2><p>EFSA identified a lowest observed adverse effect level of <strong>1 mg of glycoalkaloids per kilogram of body weight per day</strong>. A 70 kg adult would reach the same dose-per-body-weight at 70 mg, while a 20 kg child would reach it at 20 mg. This is not a recommended intake or a home safety threshold; it simply illustrates why body size matters when exposure is considered.</p><h2>Greening and sprouting are related but not identical</h2><p>Light primarily drives greening, while age and storage conditions can encourage sprouting. A potato can be green without long sprouts, or sprouted without obvious green tissue. When both appear together—especially with softness or shriveling—the case for discarding becomes stronger.</p><h2>Preventing green potatoes</h2><p>Keep potatoes protected from light, including strong kitchen lighting and sunny counters. A dark, dry, well-ventilated storage place is better than a transparent bowl left in daylight. Buy amounts that can be used before quality deteriorates.</p><h2>Bottom line</h2><p>The green color itself is harmless chlorophyll, but it is a useful warning that the potato has been exposed to conditions that can also increase glycoalkaloids. Because the concentration cannot be judged from color and cooking reductions are highly variable, a clearly green potato is better discarded than “rescued” by peeling or frying.</p>"""
}

for number, addition in ADDITIONS.items():
    matches = list(ROOT.glob(f'{number:03d}-*.json'))
    if len(matches) != 1:
        raise SystemExit(f'Article {number}: expected one file, found {len(matches)}')
    path = matches[0]
    data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
    html = data['content_html']
    marker = '<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>'
    if marker not in html:
        raise SystemExit(f'{path}: FAQ marker not found')
    if addition not in html:
        html = html.replace(marker, addition + marker)
    data['content_html'] = html
    path.write_text(json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + '\n', encoding='utf-8')

# Require every English article in the approved set to have standalone depth.
short = []
for path in sorted(ROOT.glob('*.json')):
    data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
    length = len(data.get('content_html', ''))
    if length < 2200:
        short.append(f'{path}:{length}')
if short:
    print('\n'.join(short))
    raise SystemExit('EN_STANDALONE_DEPTH_AUDIT=FAIL')
print('EN_STANDALONE_DEPTH_AUDIT=PASS')
print('EN_ARTICLES_AT_LEAST_2200_CHARS=25')
